Finding {
    layout: horizontal;
    height: auto;
}

/* Overrides two of Textual's own built-in `ListView` highlight rules (`_list_view.py`'s
DEFAULT_CSS: the blurred `& > ListItem.-highlight` and the focused `&:focus { & >
ListItem.-highlight }`) -- both set `background`/`color`/`text-style` from
`$block-cursor(-blurred)-background/foreground/text-style`, giving the highlighted row a
solid-color fill. This box's own highlight should read via text color alone, the same blue
already used for this box's border ($primary, `findings_list.tcss`'s `FindingsList {
border: round $primary; }`, and `FindingsSuggestion`'s own `$primary-darken-1`) -- no
background fill, in either focus state. `!important` is required on every property: the
focused default rule carries a `:focus` pseudo-class, which outweighs a plain
same-count-of-type-selectors override (confirmed empirically, not just reasoned about --
see this ticket's own verification script; the identical class of problem is already
documented for `FindingsSuggestion Input`'s border/height override in this package's
AGENTS.md). `text-style: none` resets any stray bold/reverse the active theme's
block-cursor style would otherwise carry through. */
Finding.-highlight {
    background: transparent !important;
    color: $primary !important;
    text-style: none !important;
}

/* `color` alone on `Finding.-highlight` above is not enough to actually recolor this row's
rendered text (confirmed empirically): Textual's `DOMNode.rich_style` walks ancestors and,
for any ancestor whose OWN `auto-color` flag is set, replaces whatever literal `color` that
ancestor declared with an auto-contrast pick against its background -- and the built-in
focused rule's `$block-cursor-foreground` token resolves to `auto 87%` (`design.py`), so
that companion flag stays `True` on `Finding` itself in the focused state regardless of our
own literal `$primary` value there, since `!important` on a `color:` declaration only
elevates the `color` key, not its `auto-color` companion (`_styles_builder.py`'s
`process_color`) -- there is no TCSS syntax to mark `auto-color` important on its own. The
robust fix is to stop relying on inheritance through `Finding` at all: declare `color`
directly on the two child columns that actually render this row's text, each uncontested
(neither has any other rule touching `color`/`auto-color` today), so their own literal
`$primary` always wins regardless of what `Finding`'s own ancestor step decided. */
Finding.-highlight FindingsDescription,
Finding.-highlight FindingsSuggestion {
    color: $primary !important;
}
